# November 21, 2026: Nevada, second scatter across the state (it had 20 places). Ground taken with
# tools/choose.py --thinnest (nv). The Comstock's edge (Bowers Mansion, the Sutro Tunnel, the Rock Point mill ruins,
# the old state prison), the Carson Valley (Mormon Station at Genoa, the Dangberg ranch), Lincoln County (Elgin school,
# Thompson's Opera House, the Caliente depot, the Panaca kilns), the Tybo kilns, Austin's brick church, the McGill
# drugstore, Hawthorne's shut 1883 courthouse, the Lovelock depot, and Kiel Ranch and the Boulder Dam Hotel in the south.
# Sources read in this session: Wikipedia (tools/wiki_raw.py), Nevada State Parks pages (parks.nv.gov), Washoe County's
# Bowers Mansion page, the Nevada State Prison Preservation Society's tour tickets, the Friends of Sutro Tunnel's ticket
# page, dangberg.org, the BLM's Panaca kilns page, the Tybo kilns' National Register form, the Nevada SHPO marker page
# for Kiel Ranch, the Episcopal Diocese of Nevada's church list, the City of Caliente's page, a Mineral County
# Independent-News article (2024), and Travel Nevada (state tourism office) where the runner's own page failed.
from data_b import N
from data_ma import W
BATCH={'key':'nv_nwjq','label':'Nevada: mansions, kilns, depots and a prison','added':'2026-11-21 19:28'}

TN='Travel Nevada (state tourism office)'

PLACES=[
N('bowers-mansion-nv','Bowers Mansion','Washoe Valley','nv','landmark','standing',39.28452,-119.84144,'exact','open','drive','','1863',
  'Lemuel "Sandy" Bowers and Eilley Orrum Bowers, who struck it rich on the Comstock Lode, built this house in 1863 in a mix of Georgian Revival and Italianate, designed by J. Neely Johnson, a former governor of California, with stonecutters brought from Scotland. They spent two years in Europe buying furniture, paintings and statuary for it. After Sandy died Eilley kept it going by renting rooms and holding picnics on the grounds. Washoe County owns and runs it as part of a regional park.',
  'The stone mansion of 1863 on the grounds of Bowers Mansion Regional Park. Guided tours of 30 to 45 minutes go through the house (county page). Interior fabric not checked.',
  'A Comstock millionaire couple\'s house, furnished from a grand tour of Europe.',
  'Bowers Mansion Regional Park (Washoe County Regional Parks and Open Space), on Old U.S. Highway 395 in Washoe Valley between Reno and Carson City. Tours begin on the hour; adults $10, seniors and children 6 to 17 $7, 5 and under free; no reservations, tickets go on sale ten minutes before each tour. The house closes for tours when air quality reaches the red zone. The 2027 dates in the hours are from the county\'s page, which says the mansion is closed for winter. Park lot off Bowers Mansion Road.',
  [W('Bowers Mansion'),['Washoe County: Bowers Mansion','https://www.washoecounty.gov/parks/specialty_facilities/bowers_mansion.php']],brick='?',
  hours='2027: Sat and Sun May 29 to Sept 6, also Fri June 18 to Aug 6; Sat Sept 11 to Oct 23; tours 11, 12, 1, 2 and 3',th=[39.28639,-119.83956],thn='Bowers Mansion Road lot'),
N('nevada-state-prison-nv','Nevada State Prison','Carson City','nv','inst','standing',39.15878,-119.73673,'site','open','drive','','1862–2012',
  'The territorial legislature set up the prison in 1862 in the Warm Springs Hotel east of Carson City, whose quarry also supplied the stone for the State Capitol. Much of it burned in 1870 and was rebuilt by inmates with stone from the quarry on the grounds. In 1924 it carried out the first execution by gas chamber in the United States. It closed on May 18, 2012, and in 2015 the legislature ordered the site kept as a museum.',
  'The stone prison buildings, shown on 90-minute guided tours by the Nevada State Prison Preservation Society that include the execution chamber and end in its museum and gift shop (tour page). Interior fabric otherwise not checked.',
  'The prison that built itself out of its own quarry.',
  'Nevada State Prison Historic Site, 3301 Warm Springs Court; tours only, run by the Nevada State Prison Preservation Society and booked through its ticket page. Tours are on set days and places are very limited; an ADA option is offered. Bring water and sunscreen. The site is not open outside tours. Lot on Warm Springs Court (the federal land map files it as a closed state prison unit; go only on a tour day).',
  [W('Nevada State Prison'),['Nevada State Prison Preservation Society: tour tickets','https://www.tickettailor.com/events/nevadastateprisonpreservationsociety/2365152'],['Nevada State Prison Preservation Society','https://nevadastateprison.org/?p=225']],brick='no',
  hours='Guided tours on set Saturdays, starting 9, 10, 11 a.m. and noon (Sept. 26, 2026 listing); book ahead',th=[39.16153,-119.73788],thn='Warm Springs Court lot'),
N('sutro-tunnel-nv','Sutro Tunnel portal','Dayton','nv','industry','ruins',39.28011,-119.58433,'site','open','drive','','1869–1878',
  'Adolph Sutro proposed a drainage tunnel under the Comstock Lode in 1860 to let water out of the flooding mines at Virginia City. Work began in 1869, the main bore reached the Savage Mine in 1878, nearly four miles from here, and the north and south branches were finished in 1879. Sutro sold out, moved to San Francisco and later became its mayor. The Friends of Sutro Tunnel are rebuilding the tunnel mouth and protecting what is left of the buildings at the site.',
  'The tunnel\'s portal and the remains of the buildings round it, under restoration. On October 19, 2026 the Friends put up a nine-foot sculpture of Adolph Sutro beside the portal (Wikipedia). Inside the tunnel not checked.',
  'The four-mile drain that let the Comstock go deeper.',
  'Owned and restored by the Friends of Sutro Tunnel, a nonprofit; open for guided tours and events only, booked through its ticket page. Travel Nevada gives tours at $65, veterans $50, children 12 and under free with a ticketed adult, and says tours usually stop for the winter after September. The site\'s own visit page would not open. Tours depend on weather and safety. Parking not checked: none is mapped.',
  [W('Sutro Tunnel'),['Friends of Sutro Tunnel: tours and events','https://www.tickettailor.com/events/friendsofsutrotunnel'],[TN+': Historic Sutro Tunnel','https://travelnevada.com/history-heritage/historic-sutro-tunnel/']],brick='?',hours='?'),
N('rock-point-mill-nv','Rock Point Mill ruins, Dayton State Park','Dayton','nv','industry','ruins',39.24861,-119.58889,'area','public','walk','','1861',
  'Built in 1861, the Rock Point stamp mill was one of the first large mills in Nevada to crush ore from the Comstock mines, and one of 21 quartz mills along the Carson River. Wooden flumes brought it water from the river. It was taken down and moved to Silver City in the 1920s, and the ground was the town dump for about 30 years. The state took the land in 1954 and opened it as a park in 1979.',
  'The mill ruins in the upper, western part of the park, reached on foot through a tunnel under U.S. 50: a concrete water tank, a reservoir and head gate, the footings of the stamp batteries, building foundations, rock retaining walls, waterways, the remains of flumes and a "hermit\'s cave", with a piece of the old U.S. 50 (Wikipedia; park page).',
  'A Comstock stamp mill, a town dump on top, and a state park on top of that.',
  'Dayton State Park (Nevada State Parks), on U.S. 50 about 12 miles east of Carson City. Open every day of the year; day use $5 a vehicle ($10 for vehicles from outside Nevada). Dogs on a six-foot leash. Swimming or playing in or near the canals and head gates is not allowed. Main entrance and lot in the lower, eastern part of the park.',
  [W('Dayton State Park'),['Nevada State Parks: Dayton','https://parks.nv.gov/parks/dayton'],['Nevada State Parks: Dayton history','https://parks.nv.gov/learn/park-histories/dayton-history']],brick='no',hours='Every day of the year'),
N('mormon-station-genoa-nv','Mormon Station State Historic Park','Genoa','nv','landmark','standing',39.00473,-119.84523,'exact','open','walk','','1851',
  'Mormon settlers built a trading post here in 1851 on the Carson Route of the California Trail, the first permanent settlement in Nevada by people from outside, at the last supply stop before the Sierra Nevada. Genoa grew round it: Nevada Territory\'s first newspaper was founded here in 1858, and the town was Douglas County\'s seat from 1861. A fire in June 1910 destroyed what was left of the trading post. The stockade standing now is a replica begun in 1947; the state took over the site in 1957.',
  'The replica stockade and wagon shed (free) and a museum in the replica trading post, with a half-mile walking path with signs on 100 years of life in Genoa. The town round it is the Genoa Historic District, with the state\'s oldest bar, opened in 1853 (Wikipedia). Interior fabric not checked.',
  'Where Nevada\'s first settlement stood, rebuilt after the 1910 fire.',
  'Nevada State Parks, in the middle of Genoa on Nevada 206. The park is always open and free; the museum is $1 (children 12 and under free). Dogs on a six-foot leash except at events; no tree climbing. Hours from the park page. Parking not checked on the ground.',
  [W('Mormon Station State Historic Park'),W('Genoa, Nevada'),['Nevada State Parks: Mormon Station','https://parks.nv.gov/parks/mormon-station']],brick='no',
  hours='Museum May to Sept daily 10 a.m. to 4 p.m.; Oct to April 11 a.m. to 3 p.m., closed Tue and Wed; grounds always open'),
N('dangberg-home-ranch-nv','Dangberg Home Ranch Historic Park','Minden','nv','landmark','standing',38.94861,-119.78944,'site','open','drive','','1857',
  'Heinrich Friedrich Dangberg founded the ranch in 1857 and built the ranch house in the early 1860s; a bunkhouse, cellar and barn followed in the 1870s, and a slaughterhouse, cook\'s house, garages and a gateway later. The family company grew it to 48,000 acres, the largest ranch in the Carson Valley, founded the town of Minden on its land in the 1900s and ran the ranch until 1978. Douglas County owns about 5.5 acres, run by the Friends of Dangberg Home Ranch.',
  'The 15-room ranch house with the family\'s belongings, and the outbuildings, among them a laundry house of 1906 and a carriage house of 1917 (dangberg.org; Wikipedia). Interior fabric otherwise not checked.',
  'The ranch family that built a town on its own hay fields.',
  'A Douglas County day-use park, run by the nonprofit Friends of Dangberg Home Ranch. Its page says visitors may walk through the ranch house on Open House days or book a private tour; the dates are on its calendar, not checked. Parking not checked on the ground.',
  [W('Dangberg Home Ranch Historic Park'),['Friends of Dangberg Home Ranch','https://dangberg.org/']],brick='?',hours='?'),
N('elgin-schoolhouse-nv','Elgin Schoolhouse','Elgin','nv','civic','standing',37.35242,-114.53453,'exact','open','drive','','1922–1967',
  'Ranch children in Rainbow Canyon were taught at home until the railroad came through in 1903, and even then the nearest schools were far away. Jim Bradshaw gave seven acres of his ranch, and his son Rueben built this one-room school, finished in 1922; two years later a teacher\'s apartment was added. It taught pupils until 1967, then became a family home. It was restored in 1998 at private expense and became a state historic site in 2005.',
  'The one-room schoolhouse of 1922 restored to its original appearance, with its teacher\'s quarters (Wikipedia; park history). Interior fabric not checked.',
  'A one-room ranch school at the end of 36 miles of gravel.',
  'Nevada State Parks, run from Kershaw-Ryan State Park (775-726-3564). Museum $3, children 12 and under free. Reached by 36 miles of graded gravel (Kane Springs Road) from U.S. 93 with no services; not suited to trailers or motor homes. Dogs on a six-foot leash outside the school. Hours over Thanksgiving and Christmas may vary.',
  [W('Elgin Schoolhouse State Historic Site'),['Nevada State Parks: Elgin Schoolhouse','https://parks.nv.gov/parks/elgin-schoolhouse'],['Nevada State Parks: Elgin history','https://parks.nv.gov/learn/park-histories/elgin-schoolhouse-history']],brick='?',
  hours='From Sept. 17: Thursday to Sunday 10 a.m. to 3 p.m.'),
N('thompsons-opera-house-nv','Thompson\'s Opera House','Pioche','nv','civic','standing',37.93011,-114.45098,'exact','view','walk','','1873',
  'Aleck Brown opened this wood-frame hall in September 1873 as a meeting house, dance hall and theatre. Alexander Thompson bought it in 1891 and enlarged the stage; it showed its first silent film in 1915 and was renamed the Gem in 1935, but in 1937 a new brick Gem Theater was built next door for films and the old house closed in the 1940s. Lincoln County restored it in 2009 and it holds concerts and community events.',
  'The two-storey frame building with a shallow Greek Revival pediment on the front, joined to the 1937 Gem Theater (Wikipedia). Interior fabric not checked.',
  'Pioche\'s 1873 stage, still in use after 150 years.',
  'Owned by Lincoln County. Travel Nevada says it is not usually open on a regular basis, gives a local telephone contact for an appointment, and says entry is free with donations welcome; it is the start of Pioche\'s self-guided walking tour. The runner\'s page (piochenevada.com) answered 403. On the street in Pioche.',
  [W('Thompson\'s Opera House'),[TN+': Thompson\'s Opera House','https://travelnevada.com/history-heritage/thompsons-opera-house/']],brick='?'),
N('caliente-depot-nv','Caliente Railroad Depot','Caliente','nv','rail','standing',37.61259,-114.51430,'exact','open','walk','stations','1923',
  'The Los Angeles and Salt Lake Railroad built this Mission Revival station in 1923 for its division point at Caliente, after the earlier depot burned in September 1921. The ground floor held the waiting room and offices and the upper floor was a hotel. The engine work moved to Las Vegas in 1948 as diesels replaced steam; Amtrak trains stopped until 1997. The railroad gave the building to the city in 1970, and it holds the city hall, library and an art gallery.',
  'The two-storey stucco depot beside the Union Pacific yard (Wikipedia; Travel Nevada). Interior fabric not checked.',
  'A Mission-style railway hotel that became a town hall.',
  'City of Caliente offices at 100 Depot Avenue; the hours are the city\'s office hours from its page, and the library and gallery may keep others (not checked). The yard beside it is an active railroad: keep off the tracks. On the main street.',
  [W('Caliente station'),['City of Caliente: services and hours','https://www.cityofcaliente.com/caliente-community/services/'],[TN+': Caliente Railroad Depot','https://travelnevada.com/historical-interests/caliente-railroad-depot/']],brick='no',
  hours='City offices Monday to Friday 8 a.m. to 4 p.m.'),
N('panaca-charcoal-kilns-nv','Panaca Summit charcoal kilns','Panaca','nv','industry','ruins',37.81311,-114.17078,'site','public','drive','','',
  'Two stone charcoal kilns high in the Cedar Range. They made charcoal for the smelters at Bullionville and Pioche, and the BLM notes that they were costly to build and needed skill to run.',
  'The two stone kilns (BLM page). What else is at the site not checked.',
  'Two stone ovens that fed the Pioche smelters.',
  'Bureau of Land Management (Caliente field office, 775-726-8100); free. About five miles north of State Route 319 on Panaca Kilns Road, passable in dry weather. Parking not checked: none is mapped.',
  [['BLM: Panaca Charcoal Kilns','https://www.blm.gov/visit/panaca-charcoal-kilns']],brick='no'),
N('tybo-charcoal-kilns-nv','Tybo charcoal kilns','Tybo','nv','industry','ruins',38.37167,-116.43028,'area','public','drive','','1874',
  'Two of the 15 kilns built in 1874 for the Tybo Consolidated Company, which burned wood from the hills into charcoal for its silver smelter. Each is 30 feet high and 25 feet across, of rock and mud, with a door at ground level, an opening at the top and a rear window reached by a ramp for wood wagons. Tybo then had nearly 1,000 people, a school, a post office and a newspaper.',
  'The two stone kilns. The 1974 Register form found the stonework original apart from some slipped rock round the lower doors, the iron doors gone, the rear windows still sealed from the last firing and the wagon ramps collapsed, inside a barbed-wire fence put up by the BLM against cattle. Their state now not checked.',
  'Two 30-foot stone beehives from Tybo\'s silver years.',
  'Federal land: the 1974 Register form was filed for federal property by a BLM conservationist, and the BLM fenced the kilns. About four miles north of Tybo, which is eight miles northwest of U.S. 6; the form says paved and graded roads reach them. Current owner and road state not checked. No parking mapped.',
  [W('Tybo Charcoal Kilns'),['National Register form (1974)','https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/74001149_text']],brick='no',conf='mixed'),
N('kiel-ranch-nv','Kiel Ranch Historic Park','North Las Vegas','nv','landmark','standing',36.20537,-115.14219,'site','public','walk','','1875',
  'Conrad Kiel settled here in 1875, and it was one of only two big ranches in the Las Vegas Valley in the 1800s. Its years were violent: the neighbouring rancher Archibald Stewart was killed in a gunfight here in 1884, and two Kiel sons were found murdered in 1900. The railroad bought it in 1903, the banker John S. Park built a white mansion, and it was later the Boulderado Dude Ranch for people waiting out a Nevada divorce. The mansion burned in 1992.',
  'An adobe building, one of the oldest in the Las Vegas area, which Wikipedia says may date from the Mormon years of the 1850s; a wooden shed called the Doll House; the ranch cemetery, reburied in the park in 2019; and an artesian well with a small wetland. Interior not checked.',
  'A ranch with two murders, a gunfight and a divorce-colony past.',
  'A City of North Las Vegas park on about seven acres of the old ranch, with lawns, paths and picnic tables (Wikipedia). The city\'s park page answered 403, so hours were not checked. State historical marker 224. Parking not checked on the ground.',
  [W('Kyle Ranch'),['Nevada SHPO: Kyle (Kiel) Ranch marker','https://shpo.nv.gov/nevadas-historical-markers/historical-markers/kyle-kiel-ranch']],brick='no',hours='?'),
N('boulder-dam-hotel-nv','Boulder Dam Hotel','Boulder City','nv','hotel','standing',35.97778,-114.83661,'exact','open','walk','hotels','1933',
  'A hotel built in 1933 for W. F. Grey for official visitors and tourists coming to see Boulder Dam, now Hoover Dam, being built. P. S. Webb, who built it, bought it in 1935 and added wings that turned its U shape into an H. Boris Karloff, Shirley Temple and the crown prince and princess of Norway stayed. It was renovated in the early 2000s and reopened.',
  'The two-storey hotel of concrete block, which houses the Boulder City/Hoover Dam Museum on the dam\'s building and the town\'s life (Wikipedia). Interior fabric not checked.',
  'The hotel built for sightseers watching Hoover Dam go up.',
  'The Boulder City/Hoover Dam Museum in the hotel is run by the Boulder City Museum and Historical Association. Travel Nevada gives it as open daily 7 a.m. to 7 p.m. and free; the association\'s own page was not found, so hours are not checked. Downtown Boulder City, on the street.',
  [W('Boulder Dam Hotel'),[TN+': Boulder City/Hoover Dam Museum','https://travelnevada.com/museums/boulder-city-hoover-dam-museum/']],brick='no',hours='?'),
N('mcgill-drugstore-nv','McGill Drugstore Museum','McGill','nv','civic','standing',39.40454,-114.77871,'exact','open','walk','','1908–1979',
  'The drugstore of the copper company town of McGill, which kept trading until 1979 and shut with its stock still on the shelves, prescription records back to 1915 among them. White Pine County bought it in 1995 from Elsa Culbert, who had run it with her husband since the 1940s, and opened it as a museum of more than 30,000 items. Its opening year differs: Wikipedia\'s infobox gives 1909, its text says the store traded from 1915, and Travel Nevada says 1908.',
  'The store as it was left: medicines and medical equipment from the 1940s to the 1970s and the soda fountain (Wikipedia). A sister of the White Pine Public Museum in Ely.',
  'A company-town drugstore shut in 1979 with everything still in it.',
  'Owned by White Pine County and shown with the White Pine Public Museum. Travel Nevada gives it as open Thursday to Sunday 10 a.m. to 3 p.m., with private tours off hours by telephone; the museum\'s own page answered 404, so hours are not checked. On U.S. 93 (Fourth Street) in McGill.',
  [W('McGill Historical Drug Store Museum'),[TN+': McGill Drugstore Museum','https://travelnevada.com/museums/mcgill-drugstore-museum/']],brick='?',hours='?',conf='mixed'),
N('st-georges-austin-nv','St. George\'s Episcopal Church','Austin','nv','worship','standing',39.49088,-117.06973,'exact','open','walk','','1877–78',
  'A Gothic Revival church built in 1877 and 1878 on a terrace in Pony Canyon, reportedly to a San Francisco architect\'s design, by the local builder Michael Finnegan with the mason John C. Wholey. Its Register listing of 2003 called it one of Austin\'s most stylish buildings of the 1800s and the only old church in the town still used by the denomination that built it.',
  'A one-storey red-brick church laid in American bond, with lancet windows and a corner bell tower with a green spire (Wikipedia; the Commons photograph in the article). Interior fabric not checked.',
  'The silver camp\'s brick Gothic church, still holding services.',
  'An active church of the Episcopal Diocese of Nevada, at 156 Main Street (U.S. 50) in Austin. The service time is from the diocese\'s list of churches. On the street.',
  [W('St. George\'s Episcopal Church (Austin, Nevada)'),['Episcopal Diocese of Nevada: find a church','https://ednv.org/find-a-church']],brick='yes',hours='Sunday service 10:30 a.m.'),
N('old-mineral-county-courthouse-nv','Old Mineral County Courthouse','Hawthorne','nv','civic','standing',38.52524,-118.62697,'exact','view','walk','','1883–1972',
  'Built in 1883 as the Esmeralda County Courthouse, it lost that role in 1907 when the county seat went to Goldfield, and became Mineral County\'s courthouse when that county was made in 1911: the only building in Nevada to serve two counties. A new courthouse opened in 1970, and the old one has stood closed since, awaiting restoration. Preserve Nevada put it on its list of endangered places in 2024.',
  'A two-storey Italianate building of unreinforced brick, with a one-storey portico, arched openings and a bracketed cornice, on a big lawn under old cottonwoods (Wikipedia). The brick is painted white, with bare red brick showing through in patches, and the windows were boarded in the article\'s photograph. It was fenced off and in disrepair in 2008; closed in 2024 (Mineral County Independent-News).',
  'Two counties\' courthouse, shut for half a century.',
  'Owned by Mineral County; closed. The 2024 article says going inside needs the county commissioners\' permission. View from C Street.',
  [W('Mineral County Courthouse (Nevada)'),['Mineral County Independent-News (2024)','https://mcindependentnews.com/2024/03/old-courthouse-named-on-preserve-nevadas-endangered-list/']],brick='no'),
N('lovelock-depot-nv','Central Pacific Railroad Depot','Lovelock','nv','rail','standing',40.17989,-118.47363,'exact','view','walk','stations','1880',
  'The Central Pacific built this Stick or Eastlake style depot in 1880 to its "Combination Depot No. 2" plan: the only one of the type in Nevada and the only survivor of six on the system, Wikipedia says. It was enlarged in 1917, and the California Zephyr stopped until 1997. When the Union Pacific planned to tear it down in 1998 it gave it to the city with the money demolition would have cost; the town moved it across Broadway and finished restoring it in 2000.',
  'The two wooden sections, a baggage room and a two-storey part with the waiting room and agent\'s rooms, trimmed with finials, braces and brackets (Wikipedia). Interior not checked.',
  'The last of six Central Pacific combination depots.',
  'Owned by the City of Lovelock and leased to shops, Wikipedia says (from a city page of 2004); whether any business is open in it now was not checked. On West Broadway.',
  [W('Lovelock station')],brick='no'),
]

